    "result_text": "All green \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, 3 tests pass (0 failures/errors).\n\n## What I changed\n\nTelephone handling now stores/returns **E.164** instead of a bare 10-digit number, and duplicate detection compares E.164 values.\n\n**New shared helper** \u2014 `rest/function/owner/E164Telephone.java`: strips spaces, dashes and brackets; keeps a leading `+` and its country code when present, otherwise assumes `+61` and drops a single leading `0` from the national digits; requires 8\u201315 digits after the `+`; returns `null` on anything invalid. So `0412 345 678 \u2192 +61412345678`, `+64 21 123 456 \u2192 +6421123456`, `12 \u2192 +6112` (too short \u2192 invalid).\n\n**`NormalizeOwnerTelephone`** \u2014 replaced the \"strip to exactly 10 digits\" logic with `E164Telephone.normalize(...)`; rejects 400 via `InvalidTelephoneException` when it returns `null`.\n\n**`RequireUniqueTelephone`** \u2014 compares the request's E.164 value against each existing owner's telephone normalized to E.164 (removed the old digit-only normalizer).\n\n**`Owner` entity** \u2014 this was the hidden trap: `@Digits(integer=10)` + `@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{10}$\")` would have rejected the `+`-prefixed value on persist. Replaced with `@Pattern(\"^\\\\+[0-9]{8,15}$\")` (and dropped the now-unused `@Digits` import).\n\n**Supporting docs** \u2014 updated `InvalidTelephoneException`'s message and the `openapi.yml` telephone schema (description, `pattern`, example) to reflect E.164; the 400/409 handlers were already correct and unchanged.",
